# Milestone: v0.9.4
# Covers: every() under an explicit tag-prefix scope (`mytag: { every (P) X }`).
#         Documents the canonical legacy "cancel-via-tag" install half:
#         the tag prefix is the cancellation target, even though the
#         .stop() side is v1.x-deferred.
#
# Companion fixtures:
#   tests/chk/reactive/every/basic.chk            — unscoped install + fire.
#   tests/chk/chunk_lifecycle/script_explicit_tag.chk
#                                                 — tag-scoped install +
#                                                   chunk-lifecycle survival.
# This fixture's distinct angle: documents the install-side of the
# cancel-via-tag idiom + names the v1.x gaps blocking the .stop() side.
#
# Reduced scope vs. the original M3 draft.  The canonical legacy form is:
#     mytag: every(P) X
#     mytag.stop()                # cancels the periodic
# Two gaps block that today:
#   1) The bare-prefix form `mytag: stmt` (no brace block) is a parse error
#      at v0.9.4 — src/parse/uparse_react.c parse_tag_prefix calls
#      parse_block(), so the prefix idiom REQUIRES a `{ ... }` body.  The
#      original urbi 2.x grammar accepted a bare statement; restoring that
#      is a v1.x emit/parse follow-up.
#   2) Even with the brace form, the identifier `mytag` must be pre-bound
#      because Tag.new() (scripted UTag constructor) is v1.x-deferred (see
#      tests/chk/reactive/tag/{tag_enter,tag_leave}.chk).  The prefix
#      scope-allocates an anonymous UTag on the cleanup stack, but
#      `mytag.stop()` has no urbiscript handle to call.
#
# Reduced behavioural assertion: the tag-prefix scope around every()
# installs cleanly and the periodic fires at least once.  The .stop()
# half is the v1.x activation target.
#
# When both gaps close, restore the canonical form:
#   var mytag = Tag.new("p");
#   mytag: every(P) Realm.fired = Realm.fired + 1;
#   mytag.stop();
#   # assert Realm.fired stops advancing across further urbi_step pumps.

var fired = 0
var mytag = nil
mytag: { every (1us) Realm.fired = Realm.fired + 1 }
0
0
Realm.fired > 0
[00000000] 0
[00000000] nil
[00000000] nil
[00000000] 0
[00000000] 0
[00000000] true
